<DocsLayout
  title="Signal Chain & Routing"
  description="How Ardor blocks connect from guitar input to stereo output, including mono/stereo rules and the Dual Rig split."
  lede="Ardor processes one left-to-right signal path. Blocks run in the order you place them, with a few rules that keep the chain physically sensible."
>
  <h2>The default path</h2>
  <p>
    Your guitar enters as a mono signal. Blocks process it in order, and a safety limiter
    always sits at the very end. A Dual Rig block can split the path into two independent
    lanes that merge into a true stereo output.
  </p>
  <SignalFlow />

  <h2>Mono and stereo stages</h2>
  <p>The chain-layout contract keeps amp and cabinet modeling physically meaningful:</p>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>NAM and cabinet blocks are mono stages.</strong> A NAM block folds its input to mono according to its input mode, and copies its mono output to both channels.</li>
    <li><strong>Cabinet blocks must precede stereo effects.</strong> A NAM block may follow stereo effects and re-folds to mono.</li>
    <li><strong>Modulation preserves the stereo field</strong> it receives.</li>
    <li><strong>Delays and reverbs</strong> keep the stereo dry signal, but sum their wet input to mono before producing their vendor-defined stereo wet output.</li>
    <li><strong>Dual Amp and Dual Rig</strong> establish stereo with hard-left and hard-right lanes.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>Input folding</h2>
  <p>The NAM block chooses how a stereo input becomes the mono signal it models:</p>
  <table>
    <thead><tr><th>Mode</th><th>Behavior</th></tr></thead>
    <tbody>
      <tr><td class="mono">sum</td><td>Average of left and right (default).</td></tr>
      <tr><td class="mono">left</td><td>Use the left / mono channel.</td></tr>
      <tr><td class="mono">right</td><td>Use the right channel.</td></tr>
    </tbody>
  </table>

  <h2>Gain staging</h2>
  <p>Three global controls frame the chain:</p>
  <table>
    <thead><tr><th>Control</th><th>Where</th><th>Purpose</th></tr></thead>
    <tbody>
      <tr><td class="mono">inputGainDb</td><td>Before NAM</td><td>Set the drive into the amp model.</td></tr>
      <tr><td class="mono">outputGainDb</td><td>After cabinet</td><td>Set the final level.</td></tr>
      <tr><td class="mono">safetyLimitDb</td><td>End of chain</td><td>Protective ceiling, default −1 dBFS. Not a tone control.</td></tr>
    </tbody>
  </table>
  <div class="callout">
    <p>
      If the sound overloads, reduce <strong>input gain</strong> first — that lowers the
      signal before NAM. If the amp character is right but the final output clips, reduce
      <strong>output gain</strong> instead. The on-device clip meter tells you which stage
      crossed full scale first.
    </p>
  </div>

  <h2>Splitting into two rigs</h2>
  <p>
    A Dual Rig block turns the single path into two independent child chains. Both lanes
    receive the same mono input; the left lane feeds the left output and the right lane
    feeds the right. Inside each lane the same ordering rules apply. Splits cannot be
    nested, and both lanes must be non-empty.
  </p>
  <p>
    Read more in <a href={url('/docs/dual-rig')}>Dual Rig &amp; Dual Amp</a>, or see every
    block's parameters in the <a href={url('/docs/effects')}>Effects Reference</a>.
  </p>
</DocsLayout>
